{4 \\left(u + 3\\right)}$ for $u>-3$ and $v > 3 + \\frac{3}{4 \\left(u + 3\\right)}$ for $u<-3.$ At $u=-3,$ the Jacobian is positive, so this line belongs to the required region.\n\nNotice that the equation\n\n$$\nv = 3 + \\frac{3}{4 \\left(u + 3\\right)}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/53/5389d4bb3047ce09fdd363b74f11656401529f5385409f693c18a1c20cac51ec.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83322a87-b177-543d-952d-ded93c066948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/86/86e54346d92ea3eca01c395db65de785091767172a164b2322bf8210ec54fdde.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541374} {:answer/id #uuid "a68cf97b-4f70-5306-aada-e38f431e0da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f4/f4c844177f02716ee92191f1452593fdaa20dd90fe2be545a794b51dfe8c0285.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541381} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e8272f-59d1-5036-8d5d-caf412d74ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b9/b99527183109e4b5f9e870ddd4f0e2738f2642b22038990272263926155e7256.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541382} {:answer/id #uuid "aafc87e3-d56f-5184-93b6-549bc04e975d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/49/49a73fc6db51b1ec1e25c47c80ff1bef122494ecb078bb1e6b120ae1341a84fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541383} {:answer/id #uuid "d08235b4-1a47-57de-83fe-b206067560d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d8/d847bbc443a2fa8ea5715c1a97b0b2c5784cba321564775c2f81a02d0d11e95b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541401}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541382} :answer-field/id #uuid "18452602-07b3-5f0d-a30a-ee8bb7add6b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c4fbb398-85ee-59ed-888e-83228cf28f5c" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\left(u + 1\\right)^{2} \\\\ 3 v + 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\left(u + 1\\right)^{2} \\\\ 3 v + 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}8 u + 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=(8 u + 8)\\cdot (3)-(0)\\cdot (0) \\\\ &=24 u + 24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 u + 24 &>0 \\\\ u > -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equation\n\n$$\nu = -1\n$$\n\ndefines a vertical line.\n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/b9/b99527183109e4b5f9e870ddd4f0e2738f2642b22038990272263926155e7256.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1745867c-2de0-5adf-8859-b6656e21d47a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a1/a14192698baa5f54314d4325bcbc3e9d488ec728b0beee1570921e0869259da3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541348} {:answer/id #uuid "5e99e166-ec0c-5b27-a479-5d9471f471ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/98/98455798ceac32138f9619758059d300b0065853da4fd4bd83af8daedc69848d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541365} {:answer/id #uuid "b90f54e8-d871-567f-b5dd-68883fc7cf68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/89/898fd878de4c411294f1949a767f28c2b5d8b2f8c4f9096b5f78835fd51c31f7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541391} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc47da7-21a7-53ca-add3-0e7ef1dfbbaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e2/e26d4947959fefd65cf852be709eaed2ac363225442686a5f160e02de1e798b3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541399} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6de183-a126-52d0-9314-0ee8f816fb1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a7/a77c173f49ff007692a1f040708adecbe65b7b035ce776faef93de1f8ce6019a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541413}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541348} :answer-field/id #uuid "996314b2-4f59-5946-8a4a-3315373fecb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c602b91c-8162-5ddc-91d0-b31b9b87f5e1" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}v + \\left(u + 3\\right)^{2} + 1 \\\\ - 3 u - \\left(v + 1\\right)^{2} - 9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}v + \\left(u + 3\\right)^{2} + 1 \\\\ - 3 u - \\left(v + 1\\right)^{2} - 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}2 u + 6 & 1 \\\\ -3 & - 2 v - 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=(2 u + 6)\\cdot (- 2 v - 2)-(1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\ &=- 4 u v - 4 u - 12 v - 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 4 u v - 4 u - 12 v - 9 &<0 \\\\ \\left(u + 3\\right) \\left(v + 1\\right) > \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $v > -1 + \\frac{3}{4 \\left(u + 3\\right)}$ for $u>-3$ and $v < -1 + \\frac{3}{4 \\left(u + 3\\right)}$ for $u<-3.$ At $u=-3,$ the Jacobian is positive, so this line is excluded from the required region.\n\nNotice that the equation\n\n$$\nv = -1 + \\frac{3}{4 \\left(u + 3\\right)}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/a1/a14192698baa5f54314d4325bcbc3e9d488ec728b0beee1570921e0869259da3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87e7020a-b4c1-5c1e-970a-f3080b2f18a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542312} {:answer/id #uuid "a21fd961-41aa-514a-84a6-bd53640d329b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542319} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb7ea70-ab48-5b5f-a35f-b10fa2c57fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-3t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542339} {:answer/id #uuid "e07e6d25-a555-5fcd-a7a4-bc176e9d0fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542341} {:answer/id #uuid "e433ebda-2236-542b-93b3-4804ba472bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542343}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542319} :answer-field/id #uuid "08cce96c-e9ed-58e4-b741-d8a8f993b454" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "02bf06bd-a114-5709-91cd-398487459c7f" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,4)$ to $(- \\frac{5}{2},2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,4)$ to $(- \\frac{5}{2},2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+4\\,j$ and $b=-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+4\\,j)+t[(-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+2\\,j)-(-i+4\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i+4\\,j)+t(-\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)\\,i+(4-2t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-\\frac{3}{2}t-1$ and $y=4-2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-2t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)+(4-2t) \\\\  & =-5t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "035fa252-c497-5441-a7a9-995b1b999642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542283} {:answer/id #uuid "1931c9f8-d41a-5ef1-b13b-ee9bd8a5e925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542289} {:answer/id #uuid "940e3279-20b0-5c97-a20b-20bc1ccb29af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542313} {:answer/id #uuid "c5abffee-6393-58b4-acdb-d0f5a1c85331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542331} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb0947a-9d73-5217-8106-27e3a8efa0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542338}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542338} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fbe6447-d7b2-5aa3-832a-868998a34289" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2fca5dad-31ea-55b7-94df-b638c15ad9f2" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,2)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},0)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,2)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},0).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+2\\,j$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}\\,i.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j)+t[\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-(-i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j)+t(\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)\\,i+(-2t+2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=\\frac{5}{2}t-1$ and $y=-2t+2$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2t+2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1+(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "473e8524-7399-51a9-981f-c0aa4668dd93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542297} {:answer/id #uuid "c04afd16-987b-5ec2-af26-9f9b902a2206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542328} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d23fa3-0d57-5274-baa4-eef4412f5a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{577}{36}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542333} {:answer/id #uuid "d58a6523-8175-5758-9a7a-5450d554c95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542337} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ab0414-6ef1-5f05-b520-6b9c768d227c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-24t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542350}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542328} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d4fa989-2ac8-5a30-824a-30041569e8ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4b732e53-9da1-5d2f-ab3b-dfb9b5fc9cae" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(3,- \\frac{7}{2})$ to $(-1,- \\frac{11}{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(3,- \\frac{7}{2})$ to $(-1,- \\frac{11}{3}).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=3\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j$ and $b=-i-\\frac{11}{3}\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(3\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)+t[(-i-\\frac{11}{3}\\,j)-(3\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)] \\\\  & =(3\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)+t(-4\\,i-\\frac{1}{6}\\,j) \\\\  & =(-4t+3)\\,i+(-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-4t+3$ and $y=-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4t+3)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}t)j \\\\  & =-4\\,i-\\frac{1}{6}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+\\left(- \\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(-4t+3)^{2}-(-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}t) \\\\  & =16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542305 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b20b7f03-488d-57c7-b9f2-d236742a9961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-3t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542324} {:answer/id #uuid "b77786f1-adbc-5af1-8f60-843037465c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{7}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542326} {:answer/id #uuid "cc8d6535-5549-51c3-b2b4-d17b02464990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542332} {:answer/id #uuid "f5361eea-4400-528a-850e-0ba9c82a1977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542347} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1096ed-014c-57c2-ab1e-36b976f89bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{277}{36}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542351}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542332} :answer-field/id #uuid "03e14137-0d58-55a7-bd84-61bca100a01f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "621c7226-fc49-5cd6-ab81-d4bf845859cb" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,\\frac{13}{3})$ to $(- \\frac{5}{2},2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,\\frac{13}{3})$ to $(- \\frac{5}{2},2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j$ and $b=-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j)+t[(-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+2\\,j)-(-i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j)+t(-\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-\\frac{7}{3}\\,j) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)\\,i+(\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-\\frac{3}{2}t-1$ and $y=\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-\\frac{7}{3}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{2}+\\left(- \\frac{7}{3}\\right)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)+(\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17c87f5e-5e1e-533e-a685-95f4e3d70e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542288} {:answer/id #uuid "40fc5e25-69f2-53fc-bfbb-ac186ba68379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{4}{3}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542295} {:answer/id #uuid "c42403a1-9e8a-5de5-882b-c347f626e69b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542329} {:answer/id #uuid "e3968766-f69b-5abb-9c51-38782ded432b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{289}{36}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542342} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b91717-1749-5c7a-be22-16af96b4ab7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542344}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542344} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f19d3c7-bdba-598e-85e9-a9f2767c5565" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a221b842-bd46-5723-b036-3a6f86665976" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{13}{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{13}{3}).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+3\\,j$ and $b=\\frac{7}{2}\\,i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t[(\\frac{7}{2}\\,i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j)-(i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t(\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,i+(3+\\frac{4}{3}t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+\\frac{5}{2}t$ and $y=3+\\frac{4}{3}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\frac{5}{2}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3+\\frac{4}{3}t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+{\\frac{4}{3}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1+\\frac{5}{2}t)+3+\\frac{4}{3}t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\cdot \\frac{17}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1474a45f-3cb5-5c5c-82a7-b35bad7ae911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{5}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542287} {:answer/id #uuid "30335a9e-1e6a-5240-a835-635bf13e5660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542292} {:answer/id #uuid "63e0ed3c-b3f8-5a81-b935-2f28c7723aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542307} {:answer/id #uuid "ae88aef5-06bc-5eb8-bf58-ee6e0e624524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-12t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542323} {:answer/id #uuid "f3aa4113-237e-5e1a-ac67-eb9f38bf7ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542346}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542292} :answer-field/id #uuid "62aaa0dc-bb9c-53ef-9689-89144abe766e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b2a5ec51-bad1-5473-9954-53b213963bd6" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-2,- \\frac{7}{2})$ to $(1,-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-2,- \\frac{7}{2})$ to $(1,-1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-2\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j$ and $b=i-j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-2\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)+t[(i-j)-(-2\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-2\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)+t(3\\,i+\\frac{5}{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =(3t-2)\\,i+(-\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=3t-2$ and $y=-\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i+\\frac{5}{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t-2)^{2}-(-\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}t) \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5582abdc-4614-5e71-964a-2d19582d849c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542300} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7b5275-0ea4-503c-a862-6c7508804d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542309} {:answer/id #uuid "70d93491-99f0-5bd9-b153-73c0784e2eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542310} {:answer/id #uuid "9acb3c73-570d-5433-98be-243f021d0f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542317} {:answer/id #uuid "9cad3f1b-7cf7-5209-ab65-767609942832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542318}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542317} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5407b6a-3e15-5951-adb6-f6a3f6d57b0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "beef72c2-a71c-5197-903a-c3c1f057b873" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(\\frac{7}{2},4)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(\\frac{7}{2},4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+3\\,j$ and $b=\\frac{7}{2}\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t[(\\frac{7}{2}\\,i+4\\,j)-(i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t(\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,i+(3+t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+\\frac{5}{2}t$ and $y=3+t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\frac{5}{2}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3+t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1+\\frac{5}{2}t)+3+t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11429afc-455f-5c8a-a4df-3cac6e918fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{325}{36}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542286} {:answer/id #uuid "51f0679e-81ac-586f-9a56-1373b928161b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542299} {:answer/id #uuid "59abc310-6605-56a9-9327-a4ec3f9272b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542302} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8df974-825a-516c-b007-9a07e0e2b6fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542303} {:answer/id #uuid "f06333fd-751a-5534-ab2f-57975ac048a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542345}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542345} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a67bdf-986e-5011-ab4a-8e0c1bca5fa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c47f3d69-bc6d-5af9-a7d6-c2e7d42c806b" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,2)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,2)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{3}).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+2\\,j$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j)+t[(\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j)-(-i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j)+t(\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-\\frac{5}{3}\\,j) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)\\,i+(-\\frac{5}{3}t+2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=\\frac{5}{2}t-1$ and $y=-\\frac{5}{3}t+2$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{5}{3}t+2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-\\frac{5}{3}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+\\left(- \\frac{5}{3}\\right)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1+(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{5}{3}t+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\cdot \\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44bb6d25-cb28-57c5-9bd8-8b5a3ea0d8a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{3}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542296} {:answer/id #uuid "69e479cd-9bf0-5481-afeb-58ec5059536d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542308} {:answer/id #uuid "9492d2db-0d16-5473-bbf2-b5753ebbfbd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542314} {:answer/id #uuid "9a14126d-fa91-5d78-8230-6c1ec9664546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542316} {:answer/id #uuid "f65df61c-e6ae-5911-bed9-45d693eec2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542349}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542316} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9fcc1cb-695e-577d-916f-00bdd3ab93a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d489cb8a-37ac-5057-8882-4a94eb183baf" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},1)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0+t\\,(b-0) \\\\  & =t(b) \\\\  & =t(\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}t\\,i+t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=\\frac{3}{2}t$ and $y=t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{2}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{3}{2}}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{\\frac{3}{2}t+t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24fc2385-2b81-5188-bb65-8be9a6b72cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542290} {:answer/id #uuid "31d095c6-9894-5dcd-a47c-fbff4a3ac7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{3}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542293} {:answer/id #uuid "9972107c-2631-5571-a39a-29d7ef026be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{4}{3}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542315} {:answer/id #uuid "a98fbfc4-3c83-5f2b-abab-ba3e760107fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542321} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ee897a-b9ef-5dd3-8d4a-48a525540411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{145}{36}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542321} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfdb0a89-889d-56b9-a2eb-f7bf05ae6bc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d4f4de30-da30-5755-a176-314836964c15" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{4}{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{4}{3}).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0+t\\,(b-0) \\\\  & =t(b) \\\\  & =t(\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}t\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=\\frac{3}{2}t$ and $y=\\frac{4}{3}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{2}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{3}{2}}^{2}+{\\frac{4}{3}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{145}{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{\\frac{3}{2}t+\\frac{4}{3}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "317cc575-d6d9-5bf7-b402-e9f6f2aece5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543849} {:answer/id #uuid "37d68e3d-f8d6-5f13-a869-d9cdc65b796c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543852} {:answer/id #uuid "7693e799-17b4-580e-85ba-48cd63b488fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$ and $\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543867} {:answer/id #uuid "e77191fb-4308-5ae9-92dc-890558829810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{4}$ and $\\frac{2}{9}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543899} {:answer/id #uuid "f6547e1e-09da-5e3a-9109-8fd09fcf5409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543901}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543849} :answer-field/id #uuid "6254bf33-2529-515f-a80a-8ecdfac48523" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "071541e3-e659-523c-b1f4-eade008267f7" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{5}{4}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{2}{9}}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{4}$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{2}{9}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{4},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{2}{9},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{9}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\sqrt{5},$ and the minor axis has length $$\n\\displaystyle 2b=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce4db2e6-57cc-53c9-a74c-1ffe1f592efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{70}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543890}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543890} :answer-field/id #uuid "9736611d-7a2c-5da0-acc9-2bf3c1d58488" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543876} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbc2dfdf-cb86-5fc6-aae5-463332bc08d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{82}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543885} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6df07a4-3e4e-56fb-903b-6dca2e87c255" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3a6ef55b-f35c-5e25-8e8b-c766bf7961eb" :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{82}{9}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{70}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{82}{9}$ and ${b}^{2}=70.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{82}{9},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\sqrt{\\frac{82}{9}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=70,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{70}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=\\sqrt{70},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{70}=2\\sqrt{70},$ and the minor axis has length $$\n\\displaystyle 2a=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{3}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{82}}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf7d248d-e685-5e26-ac25-a7926e2cbb3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543891} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa5ddd2d-b3d6-55bf-8001-4f05c1087233" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543879} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae9ca8da-f70b-516d-94a6-97e74c60595b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543881}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543881} :answer-field/id #uuid "f905f086-bf87-543a-a849-4cd7d74a502f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3f4b4f77-3a6a-59e9-8972-8eb1480893b3" :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{27}{4}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{3}{16}}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{27}{4}$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{3}{16}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{27}{4},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\sqrt{\\frac{27}{4}}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{3}{16},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{16}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\n$$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}=3\\sqrt{3},$ and the minor axis has length $$\n\\displaystyle 2b=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18c52fc5-553a-58fe-913c-0513e8e57475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543846} {:answer/id #uuid "34f14618-0ece-55f3-94b9-5cfcedbab5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{9}$ and $\\frac{5}{16}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543851} {:answer/id #uuid "8944842c-7775-566d-a53d-437f30d972f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543872} {:answer/id #uuid "b0fc53f4-2541-54f0-abf9-c06a809dfe56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543882} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c9ce9b-12fd-58ae-a2ec-ed97de9e48ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543894}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543882} :answer-field/id #uuid "789ce64e-9b22-5c24-afe7-9c36ade69dd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5c19ea88-4652-5feb-b49c-a5fe7156da75" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{5}{9}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{5}{16}}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{9}$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{5}{16}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{9},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{5}{16},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{16}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $$\n\\displaystyle 2a=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},\n$$ and the minor axis has length $$\n\\displaystyle 2b=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543859 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15738b3d-a199-50b9-9fa5-42bf040af44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543845} {:answer/id #uuid "7639f7e6-85b3-522f-bf24-1e51f41a7916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543866} {:answer/id #uuid "84ba063b-79d0-5f5c-bf77-db44f8a5f850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543871} {:answer/id #uuid "f0cc75ef-eeea-50c8-9c25-52c64d9dd650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{3}{4}$ and $\\frac{5}{9}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543900} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e25a61-f7f8-5151-9dad-062a3e7efa79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543903}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543903} :answer-field/id #uuid "96cd1554-de00-552f-a26b-2763f1e070c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5cca8aca-b32e-5ccf-8a3f-4520b45e3d49" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{5}{9}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{3}{4}}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{9}$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{9},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{3}{4},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\\sqrt{3},$ and the minor axis has length $$\n\\displaystyle 2a=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$"}]}
